azgor wrote:
> [1]: If you apply a greased figurine or stone-to-flesh a greased
> statue, it should become a greased monster. This would make the monster
> slippery and fast! "Your greased mumak called Whiskers moves like
> greased lightning!" - azgor

That's rather special-case.

> [2]: Any coaligned unattended altar should become sanctuary for a
> priest character upon entering the room. - jmr

It's raise questions as to why priest characters can't grant themselves
all the protection they want.  Anyway, I think priests are understood
in Nethack lore to be "tied" to specific temple rooms.

> [3]: Either the HoOA should have no effect on neutrals other than
> welding to your head (strict interpretation of "opposite") or it should
> have a chance of making you neutral if you are Law or Cha (loose
> interpretation). Say, 20% or so. As it stands, the only way to use
> neutral artifacts is to start as neutral. That seems imbalanced in
> favor of neutral roles. - azgor

Actually, I think neutral artifacts don't care about lawful or chaotic
wielders.  I just had an ascension with a chaotic wizard who wielded a
gift Frost Brand through much of the game without problems.

> [4]: Sitting on gold should be comfortable, and exercise charisma, when
> polymorphed into a dragon. - SpeedEvil

Too special purpose, and charisma is one of the two stats (along with
intelligence) that exist outside the exercise system.

> [5]: Since it is well known that early religious practices of some
> tribes used hallucinatory drugs as a means to dealing with the divine,
> taking a hallucinogen should give you temporary telepathy or
> clairvoyance if you are a caveman. - aristotle73

This is interesting.

> [6]: There should be a chance of losing worn rings down a sink when you
> quaff from one. -azgor

I'd say only if your luck is negative.  The player should also get the
ID effect from the sink when that happens.

> [7]: a magic marker should work like a ?oScare Monster for paper golems
> (how about a scroll of fire? -ed.) - braindancer

Why would a paper golem be afraid of being written on?

> [8]: Blessing a blessed item/cursing a cursed item should yield a(n
> un)holy item, altering its characteristics further where applicable.
> Water could only be blessed or cursed once, of course. - Grunt

In effect, this idea is suggesting more extreme blessing states: holy =
super blessing, unholy = super cursing.  This would be a major can of
worms: all the items that have blessing and curse effects would have to
have further effects invented for them, which is not a trivial
modification.  Not that it wouldn't be cool, but what would the
benefits be?  They'd all have to be considered for balance, too.

> [9]: Giants should be able to eat items made of bone as per "I'll grind
> his bones to make my bread" from 'Jack and the Beanstalk' - azgor

More accurately, they'd have to eat bonebread, heh.

> [10]: One mumak is to mumakil as a mimic should be to mimicid.  :)  And
> genociding mimics should further be known as mimicide. - Grunt

Yawn.

> [11]: You should be able to foo-proof any foo-proofable non-armor item
> by wielding it and reading ?oEW while confused.  This would allow you
> to make fireproof scrolls, not just wish for them. - azgor

Hmm... this is interesting, I think I like this one, although it'd
raise questions of why you can't use Enchant Weapon to protect armor.

> [12]: Rust, fire damage, rot and corrosion should affect wands and
> jewelry by making their effects intermittent. This could make it
> worthwhile to fooproof these items. - azgor

Interesting, although its suitability depends on #11, above.

> [13]: You should be able to attach a bell to a pet kitten/cat. This
> would cause monsters to be aggravated by the cat and be less likely to
> attack you. -azgor

Good luck getting it around his neck!  Anyway, if this were included it
would probably be in character for Nethack gods to be angry with you
for this.  Also note that this would be more than a trivial change to
the behavior of Nethack monsters, which do not attack pets unless they
attack first.

> [14]: Multilevel stores. - moussambani

Very difficult to implement!  The Wizard's Tower area in Genhennom has
a good number of special cases in the code to make it workable, you can
bet that it took a while to track them all down.  A similar process
would be necessary to do multi-level shops, plus  they'd have to be
implemented in random dungeon levels.  AND shopkeepers would have to be
made aware of off-level changes and not care if levels are changed
using the shop stairs, which would probably require a drastic rewrite
of the shop code.

> [15]: Breaking a wand should do damage proportional to charges+1
> instead of to charges because of the wrestable charge in a seemingly
> empty wand. - azgor

Interesting idea, although it'd also require the behavior upon breaking
empty wands to change, and it's not really that big a thing.  What
strategic options would this really add to the game?

- John H.



